# drop-guard=<Opcode>:<n> leaves out, in every function, the n-th pointer that would stand beside a narrow reference which an operation of that opcode defines. The check of references fails the build
# FORM: AsRead
# RUN: compile NarrowReferenceGuards NarrowReferencesAcrossSafepoints
# OPTIONS: references=narrow check=validate-back-end,validate-reference-guards testing=NoLayoutIsMadeSeldom drop-guard=GetSlot:0
# OPTIONS: disable=pass:InlineCalls,pass:HoistTestsIntoPredecessors,pass:ThreadJumps,pass:SinkAllocations,pass:AllocateInFrames,pass:RemoveUnreadFields
# DIAGNOSTIC: NarrowReferenceWithoutPointer
# CHECK: statistic NarrowReferenceGuards 0
# PROGRAM-OF: narrow-reference-has-pointer-over-call.ir
